 can pull out the  description not only of that indicator  but also how it fits into the larger  targets and goals  now with TB data when we're looking at  that TB that it doesn't just report a  single figure when you're looking at the  the incidence per 100,000 instead they  have high bounds low bounds and mid  bounds so we want to pull out just those  mid bounds and with that just like we  did with our life expectancy now we can  create a geographic visualization where  you see the tuberculosis is it's quite  an issue in southern Africa Canada in  the u.s. we're gonna do okay at the end  of the day what makes this linking  between your own data public data and  the Wolfram data so helpful is in in a  couple lines of code you can pull out  that chart that's actually going to show  you not only the TB rate that we got  from the UN but also also the life  expectancy rate for each country looking  at that from a global perspective again  using the the Wolfram technique of  natural language we can simply ask it  what is the what is the global life  expectancy for the entire world with  that we've got a chart that not only  shows us what the TB rate is which I'm  guessing most people don't know the diff  it's between a rate of 10 and a rate of  100 in terms of is that good or bad but  we can see how that rate corresponds to  life expectancy and then show it as a  percentage of the overall global life  expectancy so in Switzerland they have a  hundred their life expectancy is a  hundred and fourteen percent of the  global average and they only have a TB  rate of 7.8 and where I'm coming from is  showing 400 and has actually one of the  lowest life expectancies in the country  at less than 70% of the global average  and these are just examples that that we  are showing is kind of a one-time  through of how we're using our  information the same idea of creating  relational data sets with either your  own into these stores or wolfram  entities can be used in many ways now  I'm going to quickly go over this if  this is something you're interested in  you're more than welcome to dig a bit  into the code the the original  presentation is we're going through the  example I had the long code written out  but one of the things we wanted to do is  actually be able to automate a lot of  these processes so we created a couple  defined functions that were specifically  set up to help us create these  relational data sets the first ones very  easy simply creates flat data returns  flat data into a data set we can then  take a list and turn it into entities  that can either be your own entities or  because it uses the interpreter function  you can also turn that into say company  data using that that same code you can  then use data set entity replace so say  you you scrape a table from Wikipedia  about the top 50 countries our companies  in China you can use this to then  replace that flat data with the company  data also what you can do is if you  don't want to do a replace which is what  I'm showing below you can append an  entity to your existing data set so here  in our scg index we had all of the  information about Afghanistan but what  I've done is I've actually added the  entity Afghanistan is the first column  so again that allows you to dig deeper  and do multi-layer analysis where you're  pulling in data not only from the  country it's  self in the Wolfram repository but also  from the information that you're  bringing to the table so to close if you  look at the conclusions that we've come  to in doing this work around the SDGs  we really have found that using entities  as properties and and having that multi  layer aspect really allows you to not  only to move away from flat data and  into a relational context we found that  the Wolfram environment compared to  something like Excel is very powerful  for parsing and analyzing complex data  that's coming in from multiple sources  and as I said before we find that  relatively easily users can combine this  public data with the Wolfram data to  create multi-dimensional analysis now  some of the challenges we've run into  the automation of the process does  require that your data is relatively  clean to begin with we're fortunate that  you end data tends to be a little bit  cleaner than some other sources it's not  perfect but at least they try to be  consistent but we have run into issues  in the past another thing when you're  looking at data storage management  access distribution our team is spread  across four states and so trying to  coordinate the different findings that  we have can be a little bit difficult  when you're losing using some of the  cloud-based solutions so a 300 megabyte  CSV is manageable on your computer in  Excel but by the time you convert it  into a relational data set and start  running your analysis on that if that  becomes a cloud object we run into some  issues with that also we found that the  Wolfram interpreter function is not  flawless there's times where we have a  few results coming back that aren't  linking us to the data that we expect in  terms of opportunities and next steps  one of the things that we want to do is  to explore integrating multiple data  sources into a single a single unit and  so here I've shown our sample SDG data  and I've shown the SDG index data and  I've shown that we have the TB data  ideally we want to be able to package  that so it's all one piece that can be  easily accepted the other thing and  we've actually been in conversations  this week about it we want to continue  to look at some of what's considered  gold stay  data sources and to be able to compare  it to what's in the Wolfram repository  to either update and provide that  information to others who may find it  useful or also to understand where  there's gaps when we are pulling in  external data I'm one of the people that  has sat in and listened to Steven  Wolfram's  live seeing CEO e twitch and YouTube  sessions and one of the things they've  been talking about that got me excited  sending emails it's all hours of the  night is something they're talking about  related to entity extend I'm speaking  simply from what I've heard in the  discussions but at the end of the day  you're looking at at being able to  append additional properties to existing  entities which for us we're having a  workaround but that's exactly where  we're wanting to go and also for those  of you that were in Stevens keynote at  the beginning of the week when he popped  up his database connection to an SQL  that automatically converted everything  into an entity store I almost fell out  of my chair because that was a huge step  forward when you look at where local  data is where public data is and what it  means for being able to build these  these relational connections I think we  have a few minutes for questions if  there's anything from the crowd we've  put our information up on the screen  we'd love to be in touch feel free to  use our code or retouches
 

